    <DataNode elementId="Ubiquinones" textLabel="a ubiquinone" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="16389" dataSource="ChEBI" />
      <Graphics centerX="1144.428955078125" centerY="1096.265869140625" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="SRI, caspi">The ubiquinones are a group of lipid-soluble benzoquinones involved in electron transport. Their structures have a variable length side chain, which consists of one to twelve mono-unsaturated &lt;I&gt;trans&lt;/I&gt;-isoprenoids units, with ten units being the most common in animals. |FRAME: TAX-4932| has 6 units, |FRAME: TAX-303| has 7, |FRAME: TAX-83333| has 8, |FRAME: TAX-10090| has 9, and |FRAME: TAX-9606| has 10 |CITS: [16233343]|. The terms ubiquinone/ubiquinol refer to the oxidized/reduced forms, and are often used interchangeably. The forms are readily interconvertable. Ubiquinone is also known as coenzyme Q, or just Q. The names are often followed by the number of the prenoid units in the side chain (e.g. Q-8), or the number of carbons in the chain. For example, the name Q-8 may be substituted with ubiquinone(40). Since these carbons occur in repeating units of five, these number are multiples of 5.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="Ubiquinones" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Seed" value="cpd28301" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Metanetx" value="MNXM176" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_LigandCpd" value="C00399" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Chebi" value="16389" />
